WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.679
Okay, so for the final part of day one of week one, I want to take you through the scene, the, the,

00:07.679 --> 00:14.320
what, what is this all about anyway? And it begins with this tweet that is again from Andre.

00:14.320 --> 00:20.559
I mentioned that he's the guy that coined the term "vibe coding" and this is his seminal tweet

00:20.559 --> 00:28.679
from some time ago from like more than a year ago announcing announcing this idea that he calls vibe coding.

00:28.679 --> 00:30.960
Were you fully given to the vibes,

00:30.960 --> 00:35.960
embrace the exponentials and forget the code even exists?

00:36.479 --> 00:40.600
And he describes LLM's getting too good.

00:40.600 --> 00:44.240
So he goes through this idea that you sort of give up

00:44.240 --> 00:45.840
looking at all of the code,

00:45.840 --> 00:47.640
you ask an LLM to generate something

00:47.640 --> 00:52.000
and you let it be and come back and see it doing what it does.

00:52.000 --> 00:59.000
And this really gave rise to the term "vibe coding" and to everything that transpired in 2025.

00:59.000 --> 01:04.000
And the year ahead was then something of an emotional roller coaster.

01:04.000 --> 01:08.000
I feel like initially people were quite surprised by what these things could do.

01:08.000 --> 01:10.719
And then there was a period of sort of denial,

01:10.719 --> 01:16.159
whenever I'm pushed back a bit, and then astonishment, we all went through and we tried it ourselves

01:16.159 --> 01:22.079
and saw what could really happen. But then that was replaced by a sense of frustration that some

01:22.079 --> 01:28.319
things were working out. And to be honest, then real anger, like I said on one of my courses before,

01:28.319 --> 01:36.200
that there was a point when I found myself getting so frustrated with called code that I was I was starting to yell at it and ask it to write

01:36.200 --> 01:40.180
performance reports so I would have it read in about the repeated mistakes it

01:40.180 --> 01:44.319
was making. It was some strange thing where you would have such an identity with

01:44.319 --> 01:50.319
the AI you're working with that it could become extremely frustrating and then

01:50.319 --> 01:55.719
there was a period of acceptance when with the people that were using this every day,

01:55.719 --> 01:58.459
like myself, started to get familiar with,

01:58.459 --> 02:00.799
where is it good and where is it weak?

02:00.799 --> 02:03.200
And how do we make the most out of the strengths

02:03.200 --> 02:07.760
without getting frustrated by some of the downsides

02:07.760 --> 02:11.479
of working with AI code generators.

02:11.479 --> 02:14.520
But then there was something of an inflection point

02:14.520 --> 02:19.580
late 2025 with, I guess guess Claude Sonnet 4.5 or Claude

02:19.580 --> 02:24.740
Opus 4.5 more like that. I think that was the real inflection point followed by Gemini

02:24.740 --> 02:34.120
3 and GPT 5.2 when suddenly these AI agents got a lot better and a lot of the of the anger

02:34.120 --> 02:39.159
and frustration it's it's still times when I get impatient and when I get irritated, but

02:39.159 --> 02:45.159
there's much less of the super frustrating repeated mistakes that we used to see earlier

02:45.159 --> 02:46.400
last year.

02:46.400 --> 02:51.560
And a lot of us have seen this inflection point and that really caused it in December

02:51.560 --> 03:03.080
and January, this huge new wave of progress and this ability to do so much with AI agents. And the terminology around this space

03:03.080 --> 03:04.879
is still something that's up in the air.

03:04.879 --> 03:07.840
You hear people using different expressions

03:07.840 --> 03:09.039
to mean different things.

03:09.039 --> 03:11.000
As I say, the first one was Vibe Koda,

03:11.000 --> 03:13.039
which was Andre's term.

03:13.039 --> 03:14.520
And sometimes people just mean

03:14.520 --> 03:17.400
that is a sort of quite amateurish approach

03:17.400 --> 03:19.319
of just sort of letting something go

03:19.319 --> 03:20.759
and then throwing it away if it doesn't work

03:20.759 --> 03:21.840
or trying it again.

03:21.840 --> 03:25.879
For some people, Vibe coding is just the name of this whole field.

03:25.879 --> 03:31.240
Then vibe engineer was this term that Simon Willison, I'll show you some of his posts later,

03:31.240 --> 03:35.960
he came up with this term to represent the more professional side of it of actually building

03:35.960 --> 03:40.439
software as a professional but using AI agents.

03:40.439 --> 03:47.419
And then a new term that sort of emerged for people using this as experts is an agentic coder.

03:47.419 --> 03:50.900
That is someone that is using things like the cursor agent

03:50.900 --> 03:53.539
that we just saw as a sort of sidekick

03:53.539 --> 03:56.439
to collaborate with, to build a product together,

03:56.439 --> 03:59.300
or potentially multiple agents as we will.

03:59.300 --> 04:01.300
All right, I don't wanna confuse you horribly.

04:01.300 --> 04:02.560
I just wanna mention though,

04:02.560 --> 04:06.860
that these words can mean different things depending on their

04:06.860 --> 04:09.759
context, and it's a bit of a muddle.

04:09.759 --> 04:14.340
When I say "agentic coder" on this course, us together for the next three weeks, I'm

04:14.340 --> 04:18.319
referring to "using agents to help you code."

04:18.319 --> 04:23.980
But you may also hear someone saying "agentic coder", referring to a coder, someone like

04:23.980 --> 04:27.899
you and me as well, who's building AI agents.

04:27.899 --> 04:30.120
That could be an agentic coder.

04:30.120 --> 04:31.519
Oh, that's confusing.

04:31.519 --> 04:34.480
And also, when you hear people talking about platforms

04:34.480 --> 04:37.920
like Cloud Code and Cursor, you can sometimes

04:37.920 --> 04:41.360
call those platforms coding agents.

04:41.360 --> 04:43.899
They are agents that write code.

04:43.899 --> 04:46.800
So depending on which way round these words are

04:46.800 --> 04:49.839
and the context they use, they could mean different things.

04:49.839 --> 04:55.160
So the key point here is that if in doubt, ask the person to talk you to to clarify

04:55.259 --> 04:57.160
what exactly are you talking about?

04:57.360 --> 05:01.279
When you say agentic coder, are you talking about someone that codes with

05:01.279 --> 05:03.519
the assistance of AI agents?

05:03.639 --> 05:06.800
Because if so, that's what you and I are going to be doing together.

05:07.000 --> 05:09.120
All right, what if that hasn't completely confused you?

05:09.319 --> 05:13.879
What I now want to do is go through some coding agents

05:13.879 --> 05:15.560
with you, some other platforms

05:15.560 --> 05:16.920
where we can work with agents.

05:16.920 --> 05:20.279
There are three different surfaces,

05:20.279 --> 05:22.139
three different ways that we can interact

05:22.139 --> 05:23.800
with these coding agents.

05:23.800 --> 05:26.259
One of them is using an IDE,

05:26.259 --> 05:28.439
an integrated development environment,

05:28.439 --> 05:31.839
a product like cursor, which has windows and menus

05:31.839 --> 05:33.120
and panes.

05:33.120 --> 05:36.699
One of them is using a plugin, an extension.

05:36.699 --> 05:40.500
Typically, it's something which is bolted on to VS Code,

05:40.500 --> 05:43.300
a super popular open source IDE,

05:43.300 --> 05:45.500
and you can get extensions to it,

05:45.500 --> 05:48.000
which allow you to work within VS Code,

05:48.000 --> 05:50.300
but be using an AI agent.

05:50.300 --> 05:53.100
And then famously, the third way,

05:53.100 --> 05:56.399
which was invented by Claude Code,

05:56.399 --> 06:00.439
is using what's called a CLI, a command line interface.

06:00.439 --> 06:03.360
And this is where you're sort of working within a terminal.

06:03.360 --> 06:04.839
You're working within something that looks like

06:04.839 --> 06:06.279
it's come from the ACs.

06:06.279 --> 06:08.680
You're working in something like a black screen

06:08.680 --> 06:11.560
with characters, and it's a bit retro,

06:11.560 --> 06:14.519
but it's turned out to be such an interesting,

06:14.519 --> 06:16.959
interactive way to work with agents

06:16.959 --> 06:18.920
that it kind of, it was almost came up as

06:18.920 --> 06:23.839
an accident through Claude Code that was a side project for a while that then just

06:23.839 --> 06:26.920
blew up and now everyone is doing it.

06:26.920 --> 06:30.899
And so this idea of sort of stepping back to the way things used to work in this retro

06:30.899 --> 06:36.040
style interface has turned out to be super productive and just a really powerful way

06:36.040 --> 06:41.759
to work and as you will see yourself. And so to give some examples of these for

06:41.759 --> 06:50.160
IDEs, there's Cursor, there's Codex from OpenAI, anti-gravity from Google, and WinSurf is

06:50.160 --> 06:56.120
another popular one. When it comes to plugins, the one that comes to mind the most is GitHub

06:56.120 --> 07:05.040
Copilot, incredibly popular. And that is also available as an extension inside VS code and that's a very common way to

07:05.040 --> 07:10.000
use it. And when it comes to CLI there is of course cloud code and because of the success of

07:10.000 --> 07:16.560
cloud code a lot of the others built CLI versions kind of taking taking a leaf out of

07:16.560 --> 07:24.560
cloud code book there's cursor CLI, codec CLI, Gemini CLI and then there are also a couple of others

07:24.560 --> 07:26.920
we'll look at, Open Code and AMP.

07:26.920 --> 07:28.720
And there are a bunch of others too.

07:28.720 --> 07:31.759
A lot of people have jumped on the CLI bandwagon

07:31.759 --> 07:33.639
because it's been super popular.

07:33.639 --> 07:37.800
And I want to make the point that this is a space that is moving so fast

07:37.800 --> 07:42.720
and there's plenty of ambiguity in terms of the terms for things.

07:42.720 --> 07:50.000
Give you an example, Claude Code itself has an extension, a plugin to VS Code and cursor.

07:50.000 --> 07:55.000
So that way you could perhaps say that Claude Code isn't just a CLI, it's also a plugin.

07:55.000 --> 07:58.000
But that's just a bit confusing, that's more of a footnote.

07:58.000 --> 08:03.000
The main point is that Claude Code is primarily a CLI interface.

08:03.000 --> 08:06.000
I will keep the resources updated.

08:06.000 --> 08:08.319
So if in doubt, check out the resources

08:08.319 --> 08:10.920
or ask me a question if you want me to clarify

08:10.920 --> 08:12.839
a new tool or something that's happening.

08:12.839 --> 08:16.360
Look, there are new tools coming up all the time.

08:16.360 --> 08:18.519
One of the hardest things in this space

08:18.519 --> 08:21.720
is being able to separate the signal from the noise.

08:21.720 --> 08:24.360
There is this tsunami of information

08:24.360 --> 08:27.480
that's coming pounding at us every single day.

08:27.480 --> 08:31.920
My social media feeds are just filled with new announcements and new excitement,

08:31.920 --> 08:37.679
and being able to distinguish between the true excitement, like when Claude Coder's release last

08:37.679 --> 08:43.279
year, separate from the sort of the talk of the town this week that will fizzle in a few weeks.

08:43.279 --> 08:48.639
There's a real skill to that, and if in doubt, then of course, ask me, that's what I'm here for,

08:48.639 --> 08:53.559
but try to develop that skill to not get distracted by all of the noise in

08:53.559 --> 08:57.600
this space, focus on the true value on things like Claude Code.

